[[File:Burawi_housing_overview.jpg|thumb|right|300px|A second-generation multi-structure high-rise cooperative (MSHRC) in suburban [[Kaysoor]], 2018.]]
Housing is a tightly controlled sector in the nation of [[Burawa]], housing is a fundamental right in the [[Constitution of Burawa]]. Housing in Burawa can be categorized into pre-socialist housing and post-socialist housing. Homes built after the [[Socialist Revolution in Burawa|socialist revolution]] in Burawa can be classified into multiple eras, but fundamentally are all very similar as housing construction and design were formalized in the [[Ministry of Housing (Burawa)|Ministry of Housing]], most homes built within a decade of each other share a similar apperance regardless of the region of Burawa, excluding regions with extreme climates and geographies that permit unique housing construction. Pre-socialist housing in Burawa varies in size, share and proportion and often is highly desired due to their non-standard or unique appearances. All housing in Burawa excluding property within the city of [[Mazar]] are owned by the central government and are leased to residents as part of the [[Burawi Housing System]].

== Housing Rights ==
{{main|Housing Rights in Burawa}}
Burawi Housing Rights are a guaranteed right of the constitution of Burawa. The housing rights are implemented as part of the [[Burawi Housing System]], a government housing scheme that all residents of Burawa are allowed to participate in. Housing in Burawa is tightly controlled by the central state, and all housings must be applied to via {{url|hom.burawa.gov.bu}}. All legal residents of Burawa over the age of 18, permit exclusions are permitted to apply for residency in Burawa. This guarantees a residency within the nation subject to household size and availability. A resident of Burawa is guaranteed to one 25m² (269 ft²) household with an extra 28m² (300 ft²) per additional member in a household, up to 7. Households in Burawa are defined as persons who are legally related to each other, such as siblings, parents, grandparents, children, [[Domestic Partnerships in Burawa|domestic partnerships]], or spouses. Once a house is claimed, it cannot be taken away from a resident unless they die, move out or the building is rendered uninhabitable by law or disaster. 

A legal residence in Burawa as of the [[2002 Burawi housing standards act]] for a single-occupancy residental household is:
* minimum be 28m² (301 ft²), 
* Contain a functional restroom with toilet and shower
* Contain a kitchen that can hold a 0.25m³ refrigerator, hob, skin and electrical hookups for a small oven
* Contain a window that can be opened to at least 3 feet of clearance away from the unit
* A minimum depth of 10 feet in both length and width for at least 60% of the unit

Burawi housing law also stipulates against exchange of money for use of property, it is illegal in Burawa to sublease, rent or trade property. It is very uncommon for people in Burawa to have unrelated roommates, but exceptions do exist for unmarried couples. The 2010's also has seen a trend of groups of young adult friend groups applying for larger cohabited spaces due to media influence from other nations. Due to the [[Domestic Partnership Act of 1996 (Burawa)|Domestic Partnership Act of 1996]], localities were able to determine what could be considered a legal domestic partnership; some urban administrations in the 2010's legalized multi-adult households were considered legal partnerships, and could allow for friend groups to apply for larger homes.

While all housings in guarunteed in Burawa, most housing in Burawa comes with maintenance fees. These maintenance fees are usually nominal and make up less than 5% of a person's monthly income. Low-income residents can apply for fee waivers but usually are directed to low, or no fee housing. Higher maintenance fee residences usually exist in older or smaller buildings due to higher upkeep costs per person.

== Housing Construction and Planning ==
{{main|Housing industry in Burawa}}
Construction of housing in Burawa is a tightly controlled sector with two government-owned corporations, [[Central Construction Group of Burawa]] and [[National Northern Burawi Construction Group]] that handle the majority of construction of residential and commercial property within the nation. All rural and urban [[Administrative divisions of Burawa|administrations]] in Burawa are required to submit a housing plan every 10 years along with their projected population growth. Population growth is calculated by looking at birth, death, emigration, existing demand for housing stock, and predicted future demand due to changes in the city's job market or major infrastructure changes. Each 230 rural or urban administrations are granted a minimum level of budget for housing to refresh old stock. However, most housing budget is given to cities that are part of the [[Administrative divisions of Burawa#Metropolitan Administrations|8 major metropolitan areas]].

== Types of Housing ==
=== Pre-socialist revolution housing ===
==== Imperial-era housing ====
The earliest set of habitable housing stock in Burawa is from the [[Imperial era of Burawa|Imperial era]]. Housing built between 1480 and 1832 makes up less than 0.1% of Burawa's housing stock. Most imperial-era structures, including housing, are no longer considered residential and have been converted into museums, cultural centres, or commercial properties.

[[File:Imperial_era_farmhouse_kushish.jpg|thumb|right|250px|An imperial-era highland farmhouse in the [[Kushish]] interior, built using dry-stone construction descended from the ''[[Kaas|kaas]]'' tradition.]]
The surviving residential stock is diverse in form: thick-walled stone farmhouses with internal courtyards in the rural lowlands, timber-and-stone monasterial housing attached to [[Burawi Catholic Church|Church]] properties, and small manors built for ''[[Hoggaanka|Hoga]]'' families and provincial administrators in the highland interior. Urban merchant houses from [[Iskhal]], [[Mazar]], and [[Samara]] — narrow, multi-storey stone buildings with arched doorways and interior light wells — survive in small numbers, though most have been repurposed for commercial use. The highland farmhouses, built from locally quarried stone with dry-stone construction techniques descended from the ''[[Kaas|kaas]]'' tradition, are among the most visually distinctive structures in the country.

Most imperial-era housing is not available to be leased through the general [[Burawi Housing System]] and is usually granted as part of either a religious claim or a condition of running an agricultural homestead.

==== Industrial-era housing ====
Housing stock from the [[Industrial revolution in Burawa|Industrial revolution]] (1832–1914) exists in the centres of many major cities and accounts for approximately 2% of all housing in Burawa. The stock consists primarily of large manor houses, {{w|Terraced houses|terraced houses}}, and large {{w|Tenement|tenement}} housing complexes. Most industrial-era tenements were originally designed as high-density multifamily dwellings, often forming part of urban slums. By the 1950s, the majority were retrofitted to meet post-revolutionary habitability standards, usually by combining adjacent units to create larger, more spacious residences.

[[File:Industrial_era_terraces_iskhal.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Industrial-era terraced houses in [[Iskhal]], characterised by arched windows, ironwork balconies, and tiled courtyards.]]
The resulting apartments are among the most sought-after in the country. Their appeal lies in non-standard layouts, generous floor plans (often exceeding minimum requirements by a significant margin), high ceilings characteristic of the era's construction, ornamental facades with carved stone detailing, and excess amenities such as multiple restrooms or dedicated dining rooms. The industrial-era terraced houses of [[Iskhal]] and [[Kaysoor]] — with their distinctive arched windows, ironwork balconies, and tiled courtyards — are particularly prized.

Demand for industrial-era housing far exceeds supply. Prospective residents frequently engage property agencies (see [[#Property agencies|Property agencies]]) to monitor vacancies and submit claims on their behalf, paying commissions that can reach substantial sums.

==== Early 20th century housing ====
Housing built from 1914 to 1932 is considered early 20th century housing and accounts for approximately 2.5% of all housing stock. With the growth in popularity of automated elevators and efficient steel-frame construction, and with Burawa's southern coast experiencing rapid population growth, buildings from this era were taller and more compact than their industrial predecessors. Typical housing of this era includes mid-rise buildings of 8–20 stories, built in concentric bands around the commercial centres of large southern cities.

[[File:Early_20c_midrise_kaysoor.jpg|thumb|right|250px|An early 20th century mid-rise in [[Kaysoor]], showing the geometric facades and curved corner balconies characteristic of the era.]]
These buildings introduced the apartment block as the standard urban residential form. Units are smaller than industrial-era housing but more efficiently planned, with dedicated kitchen and bathroom spaces. The exteriors feature art deco and early modernist influences — geometric facades, curved corner balconies, and decorative tilework — that distinguish them from both the ornamental industrial era and the austere post-revolutionary period that followed.

=== Post-socialist housing ===
After the 1932 [[Socialist Revolution in Burawa|socialist revolution]], housing designs were standardized by the [[Ministry of Housing (Burawa)|Ministry of Housing]], which formulates a standard design of buildings every ten years. Most housing built after the revolution fits within the types described below. While the structural categories have remained consistent, the architectural style, materials, and amenities of these buildings have evolved significantly across four broadly recognised eras.

==== Architectural eras ====

'''First-generation (1932–1960)''': The earliest post-revolutionary housing was designed with bold architectural ambitions — wide boulevards, symmetrical facades, and monumental proportions intended to signal the arrival of a new era. Many cities had dedicated urban designers with unique visions. These plans were largely abandoned during the [[Great Adonian War]] (1935–1946), when heavy bombing raids along the southern coast created an urgent need for mass reconstruction. The only pre-war first generation planned city that was completed was [[Kankadadka]].

[[File:First_gen_housing_kankadadka.jpg|thumb|right|250px|First-generation housing blocks in [[Kankadadka]], constructed during the postwar reconstruction period. Note the flat facades and absence of balconies.]]
Buildings from the wartime era are utilitarian: reinforced concrete frames, flat facades, small windows, and minimal ornamentation. Exteriors are typically left as raw grey concrete. Balconies are absent; cooling is provided by thick concrete walls and small ventilation grilles. Communal laundry facilities and shared bathrooms were common in the earliest buildings, though most were retrofitted with private bathrooms by the 1970s.

[[File:Second_gen_breeze_block_facade.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Patterned {{W|breeze block}} facade on a second-generation building in [[Samara]], 1974.]]
'''Second-generation (1960–1990)''': Often described as "tropical brutalist," buildings from this era retain the exposed concrete structure of their predecessors but incorporate features adapted to Burawa's climate. {{W|Breeze block}} screens, louvered walls, and elevated open-air corridors allow for passive cross-ventilation. '''Balconies''' became standard from the 1960s onward, serving as extensions of the living space — used for drying laundry, growing plants, and socialising. Facades are more textured, with patterned breeze blocks creating geometric shadow effects. Buildings are typically left in exposed concrete or painted in light colours. This era produced the bulk of Burawa's current housing stock.

'''Third-generation (1990–2010)''': A transitional period in which the Ministry of Housing began incorporating painted facades (white, cream, and pale blue became common on the southern coast), improved insulation, and air conditioning hookups in new construction. Buildings from this era are visually lighter than their brutalist predecessors. In-unit laundry connections became standard.

[[File:Fourth_gen_mshrc_exterior.jpg|thumb|right|250px|A fourth-generation MSHRC in suburban [[Badalga]], incorporating stone cladding, green roofs, and planted balcony corridors.]]
'''Fourth-generation (2010–present)''': Contemporary Burawi housing combines sustainable technology with traditional Burawi architectural elements. Green roofs, rooftop solar panels, passive cooling systems, and rainwater collection are standard. Facades incorporate local stone cladding and decorative patterns drawn from pre-socialist architectural traditions, giving modern buildings a distinctly Burawi character. Air conditioning is standard in new construction, though passive ventilation remains a design priority.

==== Multi-Structure High-Rise Cooperative ====
A multi-structure high-rise cooperative (MSHRC) is a cluster of four plus-shaped towers, typically between 30 and 55 stories, arranged around a shared '''central courtyard'''. The MSHRC is the most common building type in modern suburban construction and the signature residential form of post-revolutionary Burawa.

[[File:Mshrc_courtyard_kaysoor.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The central courtyard of an MSHRC cooperative in [[Kaysoor]], used for recreation, gardening, and social gatherings.]]
The central courtyard serves as a communal space for the cooperative's residents. Courtyards are typically landscaped with trees, gardens, and shaded seating areas, and are used for children's recreation, social gatherings, and community events. Ground-floor units around the courtyard typically house shared facilities, including laundry rooms (in older cooperatives), community halls, small shops, and maintenance offices.

Individual units open onto open-air corridors on each floor. In second-generation MSHRCs, these corridors feature {{W|breeze block}} screens that allow airflow while providing shade and privacy. Fourth-generation designs incorporate planted balcony corridors with climbing vegetation.

==== Single-Structure High-Rise ====
A single-structure high-rise (SSHR) is a single building, generally occupying the footprint of a standard city block, that tapers as it rises. SSHRs are between 16 and 40 stories and are most commonly found in established urban centres, where they are built to replace ageing pre-socialist stock. The tapered form ensures that lower floors of neighbouring buildings receive adequate sunlight — a requirement of the Ministry of Housing's urban density guidelines.

SSHRs lack the courtyard space of the MSHRC, but compensate with rooftop communal areas — often landscaped terraces with seating, gardens, and laundry facilities. Lower floors typically contain commercial or community-use spaces.

==== Multi-Structure Mid-Rise ====
A multi-structure mid-rise (MSMR) consists of groups of 8–24 story buildings arranged along a street or around a shared green space. They are typically built adjacent to SSHRs in urban neighbourhoods, where their lower height preserves sunlight access for surrounding structures. MSMRs are common in medium-density residential areas and in smaller cities that do not require high-rise construction.

Units in MSMRs tend to be larger than those in high-rises, as the lower construction cost per floor allows for more generous floor plans. Open-air corridors and balconies are standard in second-generation and later designs.

==== Service Home ====
A service home is a 3–5 story building, typically designed as a triplex or quadplex, located in smaller cities and towns with low or no population growth. Service homes are the most common housing type outside the major metropolitan areas. Their small scale allows them to be integrated into existing streetscapes without the infrastructure requirements of high-rise construction. Most service homes have small shared gardens or yards at ground level.

==== Single-Occupancy Residence ====
Single-occupancy residences (SORs) are detached homes built in rural areas of Burawa, typically sized to accommodate one large family. Most SORs are constructed for tenant farming families as part of agricultural homestead programmes and are located on or adjacent to working agricultural land. SORs are the only housing type in Burawa that commonly features private outdoor space — gardens, courtyards, and in some cases small livestock enclosures.

=== Regional variation ===

While the Ministry of Housing imposes nationwide standards for unit size, safety, and construction quality, regional climate conditions produce visible differences in housing design across Burawa's three principal climate zones.

[[File:Regional_housing_comparison.jpg|thumb|right|300px|Regional variation in Burawi housing design: southern coastal (left), Burawi Highlands (centre), and northern territories (right).]]
==== Southern coast ====
The [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] coast, where the majority of Burawa's population lives, has a hot, dry climate. Buildings are designed to reflect heat: white or light-coloured facades are standard, walls are thick, and windows are fitted with louvered shutters. Breeze block screens are ubiquitous in second-generation and later construction. Balconies and open-air corridors are oriented to catch sea breezes. Fourth-generation buildings along the coast increasingly use traditional Mesogean-Kaftian stone cladding in cream and sandstone tones.

==== Burawi Highlands ====
The interior highlands are cooler and drier than the coast. Housing here features thicker walls with greater insulation, smaller windows to retain heat, and heating systems — a rarity on the coast. Stone cladding from local highland quarries gives highland housing a heavier, more solid appearance than coastal equivalents. Balconies are smaller and often enclosed or glazed.

==== Northern territories ====
Burawa's northern territories experience a tropical wet climate with heavy seasonal rainfall. Buildings in this region are constructed with steeper roof pitches and enhanced drainage systems to manage monsoon rains. Ventilation is prioritised over insulation, with wider corridors, larger breeze block openings, and raised ground floors to protect against seasonal flooding. While the structural types are the same nationwide, northern buildings have a distinctly more open character than their southern counterparts.

=== Unit layouts ===

[[File:Unit_layout_comparison.svg|thumb|centre|500px|Comparison of typical unit floor plans across the four post-revolutionary generations, showing the evolution from enclosed rooms (first-generation) to open-plan living (fourth-generation).]]
The internal layout of Burawi apartments has evolved across the four post-revolutionary generations:

'''First-generation units''' are divided into distinct, enclosed rooms — a separate kitchen, a living room, one or two bedrooms, and a bathroom — connected by a narrow hallway. Rooms are small, with thick internal walls that served a structural role in the concrete-frame construction of the era. Windows are few and small. The layout prioritises functional separation over comfort.

'''Second-generation units''' retain the separate-room layout but are more spacious, with larger windows and the addition of a balcony accessible from the living room or bedroom. Kitchens remain enclosed but are better equipped, with standardised plumbing and electrical hookups for modern appliances.

'''Third-generation units''' introduced a semi-open plan: the kitchen and living area are typically combined into a single space, with only bedrooms and bathrooms remaining as enclosed rooms. This shift reflected changing household preferences and improvements in ventilation that made open interiors more practical.

'''Fourth-generation units''' continue the open-plan trend, with combined kitchen-living-dining areas and floor-to-ceiling windows or sliding balcony doors. Bedrooms remain enclosed. In-unit laundry closets and air conditioning are standard. Some fourth-generation designs include flexible partitioning systems that allow residents to reconfigure interior walls.

Residents are permitted to personalise their interiors freely — painting walls, installing shelving, modifying cabinetry, and making cosmetic changes. Structural modifications, such as removing or adding walls, require approval from the local housing administration.

== Mazar exception ==
{{main|Property in Mazar}}

[[File:Mazar_skyline_property.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The [[Mazar]] waterfront, the only area in Burawa where private property ownership and trading are legal.]]
The city of [[Mazar]] is the only area in Burawa where property can be privately owned and traded. This exception has its origins in the [[Great Adonian War]] (1935–1946). During the war, '''[[Manaa Kadar]]''', the leader of Burawa, secured the loyalty of Mazar's capitalist class by guaranteeing that existing land claims within the city would be honoured. Before his death near the end of the war, Kadar pushed through legislation permitting the Burawi government to sell land to private individuals — a measure intended to fund the war effort and postwar reconstruction.

The legislation was controversial and remained a source of political contention for decades. During the [[Crisis of 1962]], the government formally clarified that private land ownership was restricted exclusively to the city of Mazar, and subsequently sold a significant quantity of state-held land within the city's boundaries to replenish national reserves. Mazar consequently operates under a hybrid system in which private property exists alongside state-owned housing, and the buying, selling, and leasing of property are legal within the city limits. These transactions constitute criminal offences elsewhere in Burawa.

Mazar's property market is the only one of its kind in the country. Housing prices in the city are among the highest on the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] coast, and the market attracts both domestic and foreign investment in residential and commercial real estate.

== Housing allocation and waitlists ==

Standard post-revolutionary housing is readily available throughout Burawa. Government construction policy mandates continuous building in order to maintain a surplus of available stock, a policy which also serves as a significant driver of domestic economic activity. Applications for standard units in suburban areas and in the outer rings of metropolitan areas are typically processed within weeks.

Housing located closer to urban cores, near public transit stations, or near major amenities is significantly more competitive. Applicants for centrally located units may wait months or years. Pre-revolutionary housing — particularly industrial-era apartments — has waitlists that can extend for decades, as units only become available when an occupant dies, voluntarily relocates, or the building is condemned.

Peak demand periods occur annually around secondary school and university graduation seasons, when large numbers of young adults apply for their first independent residence. The [[Ministry of Housing (Burawa)|Ministry of Housing]] coordinates with the [[Central Construction Group of Burawa]] and the [[National Northern Burawi Construction Group]] to release additional housing stock during these periods.

=== Property agencies === 
<!-- anchor for internal link from industrial-era housing section -->

Property agencies are private firms that monitor housing vacancies on behalf of clients and submit claims when desirable units become available. Agencies typically specialise in high-demand stock, including industrial-era apartments, centrally located units, and pre-revolutionary housing. Clients pay commissions for the service, which can reach upwards of 1 million [[Hanti|hanti]] for the most desirable properties.

Property agencies are not explicitly prohibited by Burawi housing law, as they are structured as advisory and monitoring services rather than property traders. However, the industry occupies a legal grey area and has been subject to periodic government scrutiny. Consumer protection bodies have argued that the high cost of agency services effectively creates a two-tier allocation system in which wealthier residents are able to secure more desirable housing.

=== Second-home arrangements ===

A common practice involves married couples who, after a period of cohabitation, have one spouse apply for a separate housing unit while retaining their shared primary residence. As each legal adult in [[Burawa]] is entitled to apply for a unit independently under the [[Burawi Housing System]], the application itself does not violate any statute; the resulting arrangement, however, in which one spouse maintains a residence they do not regularly occupy, is inconsistent with the system's occupancy requirements.

Second units obtained through this method are most commonly located in rural areas or smaller cities and serve as holiday or weekend retreats. As the legal claimant remains on record at the address and the unit is not sublet to a third party, the arrangement is seldom detected unless the individuals are under investigation for unrelated offences.

The practice is widely acknowledged and generally tolerated by authorities. The majority of Burawi citizens secure their primary housing near their place of employment and remain at that address for the duration of their working lives; the second-home practice is consequently regarded as a minor irregularity that contributes to overall residential satisfaction. The government has not pursued systematic enforcement, though the issue has been raised periodically in debates over housing allocation reform.

== Housing culture ==

Because housing assignments in Burawa are effectively permanent — units cannot be reclaimed by the state except in cases of death, voluntary departure, or building condemnation — and because subletting and property exchange are prohibited, residential tenure in the country is among the most stable in [[Adonia]].

[[File:Burawi_apartment_interior.jpg|thumb|right|250px|A personalised apartment interior in a second-generation building in [[Iskhal]], 2021.]]
Interior personalization is common and largely unrestricted. Residents routinely repaint walls, install custom cabinetry and shelving, and modify kitchens and bathrooms. Extensive interior renovations are particularly prevalent in industrial-era apartments, where non-standard floor plans allow for significant reconfiguration. An active domestic interior design culture exists in Burawa, with dedicated publications, television programmes, and social media platforms focused on apartment renovation within the constraints of standardised layouts.

Communal life is a significant feature of residential culture, particularly in multi-structure high-rise cooperatives (MSHRCs). The shared courtyard model encourages regular interaction between residents, and informal systems of mutual aid — including childcare, meal-sharing, and elder assistance — are widely reported in cooperative housing. Neighbourhood identity is generally strong, and cooperative residents frequently maintain long-term social ties with their neighbours.

== Criticism and issues ==

=== Informal property market ===

Despite legal prohibitions on subletting, renting, and property exchange, an informal property market operates in Burawa. The most common arrangement involves a legal occupant permitting another individual to reside in the unit in exchange for payment, while retaining the official claim and maintaining residency on record at the address. The legal occupant may reside elsewhere, typically in a less desirable unit or with family members.

Enforcement has proved difficult, as the legal occupant's name remains on record and authorities must demonstrate that the registered resident does not in fact occupy the unit. The government conducts periodic residency audits and operates anonymous reporting systems, but the practice remains prevalent, particularly in high-demand areas of [[Iskhal]], [[Kaysoor]], and [[Badalga]].

=== Uniformity ===

The standardisation of housing design has been a persistent subject of public debate. Because the [[Ministry of Housing (Burawa)|Ministry of Housing]] implements uniform building designs on ten-year cycles, large areas of the country's residential landscape are visually homogeneous, particularly in suburban MSHRC developments. Critics argue that the nationwide application of identical building plans has produced a monotonous built environment that lacks regional character. The fourth-generation design guidelines, which incorporate local stone cladding, varied facade patterns, and references to pre-socialist architectural traditions, have been described by the Ministry as an effort to address these concerns.

=== Urban-rural inequality ===

The eight major [[Administrative divisions of Burawa#Metropolitan Administrations|metropolitan areas]] receive the majority of the national housing budget, resulting in newer and better-maintained housing stock in large cities compared with smaller towns and rural areas. Service homes and single-occupancy residences in rural administrations are frequently first- or second-generation construction that has not been significantly updated. The disparity has been criticised by rural residents and opposition politicians, who argue that the budget allocation formula disproportionately favours metropolitan areas.

== Accessibility ==

Accessibility provisions in Burawi housing have evolved considerably since the post-revolutionary period. First- and second-generation buildings were constructed with limited consideration for residents with disabilities; narrow corridors, stepped entrances, and the absence of lifts in lower-rise structures are characteristic of housing stock from these eras. Subsequent housing reform legislation introduced minimum accessibility requirements for new construction, including wider egresses, enlarged bathroom floor areas, and lift access in buildings above a specified number of stories.

Under current regulations, all fourth-generation housing units are required to be accessibility-ready, meaning that the unit's layout, doorway widths, bathroom dimensions, and corridor clearances meet standards sufficient for wheelchair users and residents with limited mobility. Older buildings are not required to be retrofitted to the same standard, though accessibility improvements are prioritised when units undergo scheduled renovation. Residents with documented disabilities may apply for priority allocation to ground-floor or lower-floor units with lift access.

== Student and institutional housing ==

Students enrolled in [[Burawa|Burawi]] universities and secondary schools are required to reside in dormitory housing operated by their institution for the duration of their studies. Dormitory residents are not eligible to apply for independent housing through the [[Burawi Housing System]] while enrolled, though they may do so upon graduation. The annual peak in housing applications following graduation seasons is a direct consequence of this policy (see [[#Housing allocation and waitlists|Housing allocation and waitlists]]).

Members of the military, police, and recognised religious orders are similarly housed in institutional dormitories provided by their respective organisations. Unlike students, however, these individuals are permitted to apply for additional housing through the standard system while serving, provided they meet the general eligibility requirements. Military and police dormitories are typically located on or adjacent to bases and stations, while religious housing is administered by the relevant religious institution, most commonly the [[Burawi Catholic Church]].

== Building maintenance ==

Responsibility for building maintenance in Burawa is divided between the state and the resident. The government, through the local housing administration and the two state construction corporations ([[Central Construction Group of Burawa]] and [[National Northern Burawi Construction Group]]), is responsible for the structural integrity and exterior maintenance of all state-owned residential buildings. This includes the building envelope, roof, communal corridors, lifts, plumbing and electrical mains, and shared facilities such as courtyards and laundry rooms. The maintenance fees paid by residents (see [[#Housing Rights|Housing Rights]]) fund this work.

Residents are responsible for the interior maintenance of their own units, including paint, fixtures, cabinetry, appliances, and internal plumbing and wiring beyond the mains connection point. Residents may carry out interior repairs and renovations at their own expense; structural modifications require prior approval from the local housing administration.

The condition of building maintenance varies significantly by age and location. Fourth-generation buildings in metropolitan areas are generally well maintained, while first- and second-generation stock in smaller cities and rural areas frequently suffers from deferred maintenance, contributing to the urban-rural inequality described above.

== Historic preservation ==

The preservation of pre-socialist housing stock in Burawa is not governed by a single national heritage system. Instead, historic preservation is administered at the local level, with individual urban and rural [[Administrative divisions of Burawa|administrations]] empowered to designate buildings or districts as protected heritage sites. Designated structures are subject to restrictions on demolition and exterior alteration, though the specific regulations vary by locality.

In practice, the degree of preservation activity differs considerably across the country. Major cities with significant pre-socialist stock — particularly [[Iskhal]], [[Kaysoor]], and [[Samara]] — maintain active heritage registers and employ dedicated preservation offices. Smaller administrations with limited budgets may lack formal heritage programmes, relying instead on the general desirability of pre-socialist buildings (and the resulting low vacancy rates) to discourage demolition.

Imperial-era structures of particular historical or architectural significance are in some cases maintained directly by the [[Burawi Catholic Church]] (in the case of monasterial housing) or by the [[Ministry of Culture (Burawa)|Ministry of Culture]] (in the case of former ''[[Hoggaanka|Hoga]]'' manors and other secular buildings designated as national heritage sites).

